        "root_concepts": "[french]",
        "section": "world-languages",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "French - Language Reference",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "LIAISON — breaking the silent-consonant rule\n  When word ends in normally-silent consonant and next word starts with vowel,\n  the consonant IS pronounced and links to the vowel:\n    \"les amis\" → [lezami] (s from les links to amis)\n    \"vous avez\" → [vuzave]\n    \"ils ont\" → [ilzɔ̃]\n  Liaison is OBLIGATORY in some contexts, FORBIDDEN in others.\n    Obligatory: article+noun, subject pronoun+verb\n    Forbidden: et + following word, after singular noun\n    Optional: in careful speech between adjective+noun"
    }
